For a long time, the only form of concurrency available to computer systems was
in the form of task-switching, that is, switching between N number of different
tasks at the same time, giving the allusion that the tasks were being accomplished
at the same time.
There are subtle differences between this widely accepted form of concurrency and
true concurrency, that is, operations occuring in parallel across mulitple
processors/systems. Particularly when it comes to the memory model of such systems.

We will refer to "real concurrency" as hardware concurrency.

