The naive way to take advantage of concurrency is to seperate the application in N-processes
that communicate through operating system interprocess communication primitives, such as
sockets, pipes, signals, files, etc.
however, this may come with the downside of being slow or hard to set up, as you are the whims
of the underlying operating system.
┌───────────┐  ┌─────────┐  ┌───────────┐
│ Process 1 │  │ OS      │  │ Process 2 │
│ ┌──────┐  │  │  ┌───┐  │  │ ┌──────┐  │
│ │Thread│◄─┼──┼─►│IPC│◄─┼──┼►│Thread│  │
│ └──────┘  │  │  └───┘  │  │ └──────┘  │
└───────────┘  └─────────┘  └───────────┘
There is also the inherit cost of the operating system starting and managing the processes
(Which we will come to find is larger than just starting a thread)
The upside of using multiple processes as a concurrency method is the ability to take advantage
of the OS interprocess communication mechanisms, which makes it drastically easier to write
safe code. Erlang takes this approach, and considers programs as their atomic building block.
It also makes it trivially easy to deploy the application across multiple systems on a network.

A seperate approach to concurrency is utilizing multiple threads in the same process. This has
the advantage of a shared memory space between the threads, such as shared globals and memory
addresses.
┌────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Process                            │
│             ┌────────┐             │
│┌────────┐   │ Shared │   ┌────────┐│
││Thread 1├──►│ Memory │◄──┤Thread 2││
│└────────┘   └────────┘   └────────┘│
└────────────────────────────────────┘
This allows lighter weight and faster communication between concurrent tasks, but this also
leaves safety and consistency up to the programmer. This is non-trivial but not impossible,
and different techniques may work best given the tasks at hand.

When to use concurrency:
    a) seperation of concerns
    b) performance

