Some algorithms have clear distinctions of independent work that can be parallelized, such
that as the number of threads scales the amount of work that can be done scales ~accordingly
these problems are called embarrassingly parallel. 

Some other problems might be divisible into a static number of seperate tasks.

Sometimes, we may be able to use more threads to do more of the task. e.g. writing multiple files
at the same time, or processing separate areas of a video frame.

You should not use concurrency if the overhead of managing the thread is more than the task
Threads are a limited resource, and each one takes up memory and scheduling resources
Thread pools can alleviate this issue, but they come with their own problems.

C++11 introduced classes for managing:
    <thread>             Threads
    <mutex>              Protecting shared data
    <condition_variable> Synchronizing operations between threads
    <atomic>             Low-level atomic operations
If we wish to use platform specific API's these can be can be accessed through the CPP STL
through calls to `native_handle()`, though a majority of use cases should be handled by the STL

a concurrent c++ program looks like many other c++ program, but now, we must ensure that shared
data is safe for concurrent access. we must also set up infra to even run the concurrent threads

<c++>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
void hello(){
    std::cout << "Hello Concurrent World\n";
}
int main(){
    std::thread t(hello);   // launches thread t at hello()
    t.join();               // waits for t to complete
};
</c++>
